(   - By the Mean Value Theorem, there exists c1 in \(a, a + h\) such that:) Tj
T*
(   Delta\(h, k\) = phi'\(c1\) * h = h * [ fx\(c1, b + k\) - fx\(c1, b\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Now define g\(y\) = fx\(c1, y\). Applying the Mean Value Theorem to g on [b, b + k]:) Tj
T*
(   fx\(c1, b + k\) - fx\(c1, b\) = g'\(d1\) * k = f_yx\(c1, d1\) * k, for some d1 in \(b, b + k\).) Tj
T*
(   - Substituting back:) Tj
T*
(   Delta\(h, k\) = h * k * f_yx\(c1, d1\). \(Eq. 1\)) Tj
T*
(   - Now reverse the order of grouping: define psi\(y\) = f\(a + h, y\) - f\(a, y\).) Tj
T*
(   Then: Delta\(h, k\) = psi\(b + k\) - psi\(b\).) Tj
T*
(   - By LMVT on psi, there exists d2 in \(b, b + k\) such that:) Tj
T*
(   Delta\(h, k\) = psi'\(d2\) * k = k * [ fy\(a + h, d2\) - fy\(a, d2\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Applying LMVT to h\(x\) = fy\(x, d2\) on [a, a + h] gives c2 in \(a, a + h\) such that:) Tj
T*
(   fy\(a + h, d2\) - fy\(a, d2\) = f_xy\(c2, d2\) * h.) Tj
T*
(   - Substituting back:) Tj
T*
(   Delta\(h, k\) = h * k * f_xy\(c2, d2\). \(Eq. 2\)) Tj
T*
(   - Equating Eq. 1 and Eq. 2 and dividing by h * k != 0:) Tj
T*
(   f_yx\(c1, d1\) = f_xy\(c2, d2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Take limits as \(h, k\) -> \(0, 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Since c1, c2 -> a and d1, d2 -> b, and since both fxy and fyx are continuous at \(a, b\):) Tj
T*
(   f_yx\(a, b\) = f_xy\(a, b\).) Tj
T*
(   Hence proved.) Tj
