(   where epsilon1, epsilon2 -> 0 as \(Delta_x, Delta_y\) -> \(0, 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - The linear principal part is called the Total Differential dz:) Tj
T*
(   dz = \(df/dx\) * dx + \(df/dy\) * dy.) Tj
T*
(   2. Derivation of Chain Rule for Total Derivative dz/dt:) Tj
T*
(   - Suppose z = f\(x, y\) is differentiable and x = x\(t\), y = y\(t\) are differentiable functions of) Tj
T*
(   t.) Tj
T*
(   - When t changes by Delta_t, x and y change by:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_x = x\(t + Delta_t\) - x\(t\), Delta_y = y\(t + Delta_t\) - y\(t\).) Tj
T*
(   - Corresponding increment in z:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_z = \(df/dx\) * Delta_x + \(df/dy\) * Delta_y + epsilon1 * Delta_x + epsilon2 * Delta_y.) Tj
T*
(   - Divide throughout by Delta_t != 0:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_z / Delta_t = \(df/dx\) * \(Delta_x / Delta_t\) + \(df/dy\) * \(Delta_y / Delta_t\) + epsilon1 *) Tj
T*
(   \(Delta_x / Delta_t\) + epsilon2 * \(Delta_y / Delta_t\).) Tj
T*
(   - Take the limit as Delta_t -> 0:) Tj
T*
(   Since x\(t\) and y\(t\) are differentiable, they are continuous, so Delta_x -> 0 and Delta_y -> 0) Tj
T*
(   as Delta_t -> 0.) Tj
T*
(   Consequently, epsilon1 -> 0 and epsilon2 -> 0.) Tj
T*
(   Also: lim \(Delta_x / Delta_t\) = dx / dt, and lim \(Delta_y / Delta_t\) = dy / dt.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore:) Tj
T*
(   dz / dt = \(df/dx\) * \(dx/dt\) + \(df/dy\) * \(dy/dt\). \(Proved\).) Tj
