(   [Problem Statement]: Define the Jacobian determinant for two and three variables. Give its geometric interpretation as a local magnification factor for areas and volumes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Formal Definitions of Jacobians:) Tj
T*
(   - For two functions u\(x, y\) and v\(x, y\), the Jacobian determinant is defined as:) Tj
T*
(   J = d\(u, v\) / d\(x, y\) = det\([ du/dx, du/dy; dv/dx, dv/dy ]\) = \(du/dx\)*\(dv/dy\) -) Tj
T*
(   \(du/dy\)*\(dv/dx\).) Tj
T*
(   - For three functions u\(x, y, z\), v\(x, y, z\), and w\(x, y, z\):) Tj
T*
(   J = d\(u, v, w\) / d\(x, y, z\) = det\([ ux, uy, uz; vx, vy, vz; wx, wy, wz ]\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Geometrical Interpretation:) Tj
T*
(   - Area Transformation in R^2:) Tj
T*
(   Consider a small rectangular area element in the xy-plane defined by orthogonal vectors dx*i) Tj
T*
(   and dy*j.) Tj
T*
(   Under the transformation u = u\(x, y\), v = v\(x, y\), the rectangle is mapped to a curvilinear) Tj
T*
(   parallelogram in the uv-plane spanned by:) Tj
T*
(   t1 = [ux * dx, vx * dx]^T and t2 = [uy * dy, vy * dy]^T.) Tj
T*
(   The area of this parallelogram is given by the cross product magnitude:) Tj
T*
(   dA_{uv} = |t1 x t2| = |ux * vy - vx * uy| * dx dy = |J| * dA_{xy}.) Tj
T*
(   Thus, |J| represents the local AREA DILATATION \(magnification factor\).) Tj
T*
(   - Volume Transformation in R^3:) Tj
T*
(   Similarly, a differential box dx dy dz is transformed into a parallelepiped of volume:) Tj
T*
(   dV_{uvw} = |det\([ux, uy, uz; vx, vy, vz; wx, wy, wz]\)| * dx dy dz = |J| * dV_{xyz}.) Tj
T*
(   Thus, |J| represents the local VOLUME DILATATION factor.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
ET

