BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 12:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Prove the Reciprocal Property of Jacobians: J * J' = 1, where J = d\(u, v\)/d\(x, y\) and J' = d\(x, y\)/d\(u, v\). Verify this property for polar coordinates x = r*cos theta, y = r*sin theta.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Proof:) Tj
T*
(   - In the Jacobian chain rule:) Tj
T*
(   [ d\(u, v\) / d\(r, s\) ] = [ d\(u, v\) / d\(x, y\) ] * [ d\(x, y\) / d\(r, s\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Set r = u and s = v:) Tj
T*
(   d\(u, v\) / d\(u, v\) = [ d\(u, v\) / d\(x, y\) ] * [ d\(x, y\) / d\(u, v\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Compute d\(u, v\) / d\(u, v\):) Tj
T*
(   d\(u, v\) / d\(u, v\) = det\([ du/du, du/dv; dv/du, dv/dv ]\) = det\([ 1, 0; 0, 1 ]\) = 1 - 0 = 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore:) Tj
T*
(   1 = [ d\(u, v\) / d\(x, y\) ] * [ d\(x, y\) / d\(u, v\) ] = J * J') Tj
T*
(   => J * J' = 1. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Verification for Polar Coordinates:) Tj
T*
(   - Transformation: x = r * cos theta, y = r * sin theta.) Tj
T*
(   - Compute J = d\(x, y\) / d\(r, theta\):) Tj
T*
(   dx/dr = cos theta, dx/dtheta = -r * sin theta.) Tj
T*
(   dy/dr = sin theta, dy/dtheta = r * cos theta.) Tj
T*
(   J = det\([ cos theta, -r * sin theta; sin theta, r * cos theta ]\)) Tj
T*
ET

