(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State the criterion of functional dependence of two functions u\(x, y\) and v\(x, y\). Prove that a necessary and sufficient condition for u and v to be functionally dependent is that their Jacobian identically vanishes: d\(u, v\)/d\(x, y\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of Functional Dependence:) Tj
T*
(   - Two functions u\(x, y\) and v\(x, y\) are functionally dependent in a domain D if there exists a) Tj
T*
(   differentiable relation Phi\(u, v\) = 0 such that Phi is not identically zero, but Phi\(u\(x, y\),) Tj
T*
(   v\(x, y\)\) = 0 for all \(x, y\) in D.) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof of Necessary Condition \(=>\):) Tj
T*
(   - Suppose u and v are functionally dependent: Phi\(u, v\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Differentiate Phi\(u\(x, y\), v\(x, y\)\) = 0 with respect to x:) Tj
T*
(   \(dPhi/du\) * \(du/dx\) + \(dPhi/dv\) * \(dv/dx\) = 0. \(Eq. 1\)) Tj
T*
(   - Differentiate with respect to y:) Tj
T*
(   \(dPhi/du\) * \(du/dy\) + \(dPhi/dv\) * \(dv/dy\) = 0. \(Eq. 2\)) Tj
T*
(   - Equations 1 and 2 form a homogeneous linear system in the unknowns [dPhi/du, dPhi/dv]^T.) Tj
T*
(   - Since Phi is not trivial, [dPhi/du, dPhi/dv]^T != [0, 0]^T.) Tj
T*
(   - A homogeneous system has non-trivial solutions if and only if the coefficient determinant is) Tj
T*
(   zero:) Tj
T*
(   det\([ du/dx, dv/dx; du/dy, dv/dy ]\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   => \(du/dx\)*\(dv/dy\) - \(du/dy\)*\(dv/dx\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   => d\(u, v\) / d\(x, y\) = 0. \(Necessity proved\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Proof of Sufficient Condition \(<=\):) Tj
T*
(   - Suppose J = d\(u, v\)/d\(x, y\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - If du/dx = du/dy = 0, then u is constant \(u = c\), so Phi\(u, v\) = u - c = 0 is a functional) Tj
T*
(   relation.) Tj
T*
(   - Otherwise, assume without loss of generality that du/dy != 0.) Tj
T*
(   - By the Implicit Function Theorem, u = u\(x, y\) can be solved locally for y: y = psi\(x, u\).) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute into v: v = v\(x, psi\(x, u\)\) = F\(x, u\).) Tj
T*
(   - Differentiate F with respect to x \(holding u constant\):) Tj
T*
(   dF/dx = \(dv/dx\) + \(dv/dy\)*\(dpsi/dx\).) Tj
T*
(   From u\(x, psi\(x, u\)\) = u: \(du/dx\) + \(du/dy\)*\(dpsi/dx\) = 0 => dpsi/dx = - \(du/dx\) / \(du/dy\).) Tj
T*
(   Substitute:) Tj
T*
(   dF/dx = \(dv/dx\) - \(dv/dy\) * [ \(du/dx\) / \(du/dy\) ] = [ \(dv/dx\)*\(du/dy\) - \(dv/dy\)*\(du/dx\) ] /) Tj
T*
(   \(du/dy\)) Tj
T*
(   = - J / \(du/dy\).) Tj
T*
(   - Since J = 0, dF/dx = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - This implies F does not depend on x! Thus v = F\(u\), which means v is purely a function of u:) Tj
T*
(   Phi\(u, v\) = v - F\(u\) = 0.) Tj
T*
ET

