/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the theoretical foundation of Lagrange's Method of Undetermined Multipliers. Provide its geometric interpretation using level curves and gradient vectors.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Problem Statement:) Tj
T*
(   - Optimize an objective function f\(x, y, z\) subject to a constraint phi\(x, y, z\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   2. Geometric Derivation and Gradient Alignment:) Tj
T*
(   - The constraint phi\(x, y, z\) = 0 defines a smooth surface S in R^3.) Tj
T*
(   - At every point on S, the gradient vector grad\(phi\) = [dphi/dx, dphi/dy, dphi/dz]^T is) Tj
T*
(   perpendicular \(normal\) to the constraint surface.) Tj
T*
(   - Consider the level surfaces of the objective function: f\(x, y, z\) = c.) Tj
T*
(   - As constant c varies, the level surface f = c moves through space.) Tj
T*
(   - At a constrained extremum \(maximum or minimum\) point P0 on surface S, the level surface f = c) Tj
T*
(   must be TANGENT to the constraint surface phi = 0!) Tj
T*
(   If f = c intersected S transversally, we could move along S in one direction to increase f and) Tj
T*
(   in the opposite direction to decrease f, meaning P0 could not be an extremum.) Tj
T*
(   - Since two tangent surfaces share a common normal vector at their point of tangency, the) Tj
T*
(   gradient of f must be collinear \(parallel\) to the gradient of phi:) Tj
T*
(   grad\(f\) = -lambda * grad\(phi\),) Tj
T*
(   where lambda is a scalar called the Lagrange Multiplier.) Tj
T*
(   - Rearranging:) Tj
T*
(   grad\(f\) + lambda * grad\(phi\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   3. Formulation of the Lagrange Auxiliary Function:) Tj
T*
(   - Define F\(x, y, z, lambda\) = f\(x, y, z\) + lambda * phi\(x, y, z\).) Tj
T*
(   - Taking partial derivatives and setting to zero:) Tj
T*
(   dF / dx = fx + lambda * phix = 0) Tj
T*
(   dF / dy = fy + lambda * phiy = 0) Tj
T*
(   dF / dz = fz + lambda * phiz = 0) Tj
T*
(   dF / dlambda = phi\(x, y, z\) = 0 \(recovers the constraint\).) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
