(>> Solution to Question 25:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive formulas for error estimation using total differentials. If the equivalent resistance R of two resistors in parallel is given by 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2, and R1, R2 have possible errors of 1% and 2% respectively, calculate the maximum percentage error in R when R1 = 100 Ohms and R2 = 200 Ohms.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Error Estimation via Total Differentials:) Tj
T*
(   - For a differentiable function u = f\(x1, x2, ..., xn\), the small error delta-u caused by small) Tj
T*
(   measurement errors delta-x_i is approximated by the total differential:) Tj
T*
(   delta-u ~ du = sum_{i=1}^n \(df / dx_i\) * delta-x_i.) Tj
T*
(   - Relative Error: delta-u / u ~ du / u.) Tj
T*
(   - Percentage Error: \(delta-u / u\) * 100%.) Tj
T*
(   2. Derivation for Parallel Resistance:) Tj
T*
(   - Differentiate the relation 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 implicitly:) Tj
T*
(   - \(1 / R^2\) * dR = - \(1 / R1^2\) * dR1 - \(1 / R2^2\) * dR2.) Tj
T*
(   Multiply throughout by -R:) Tj
T*
(   dR / R = \(R / R1^2\) * dR1 + \(R / R2^2\) * dR2) Tj
T*
(   = \(R / R1\) * \(dR1 / R1\) + \(R / R2\) * \(dR2 / R2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum relative error occurs when errors accumulate with the same sign:) Tj
T*
(   |dR / R|_max = \(R / R1\) * |dR1 / R1| + \(R / R2\) * |dR2 / R2|.) Tj
T*
(   3. Step-by-Step Numerical Computation:) Tj
T*
(   - Given: R1 = 100 Ohms, R2 = 200 Ohms.) Tj
T*
(   - 1/R = 1/100 + 1/200 = 3/200 => R = 200 / 3 = 66.67 Ohms.) Tj
T*
(   - R / R1 = \(200 / 3\) / 100 = 2/3.) Tj
T*
(   - R / R2 = \(200 / 3\) / 200 = 1/3.) Tj
T*
(   - Given percentage errors:) Tj
T*
(   |dR1 / R1| * 100% = 1%.) Tj
T*
(   |dR2 / R2| * 100% = 2%.) Tj
T*
(   - Maximum percentage error in R:) Tj
T*
(   Percentage Error = \(2/3\) * \(1%\) + \(1/3\) * \(2%\)) Tj
T*
(   = 2/3% + 2/3% = 4/3% = 1.333%.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The maximum possible percentage error in the calculated equivalent resistance is 1.33%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

