(   [Problem Statement]: Define the directional derivative of a scalar field f\(x, y, z\) along a direction u. Prove that the directional derivative is maximum in the direction of grad\(f\), and compute the directional derivative of f\(x, y, z\) = x^2 * y * z + 4*x*z^2 at point \(1, -2, -1\) in the direction of vector 2*i - j - 2*k.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition:) Tj
T*
(   - The directional derivative of f at point P in the direction of unit vector u is:) Tj
T*
(   D_u f\(P\) = lim_{h -> 0} [ f\(P + h*u\) - f\(P\) ] / h = grad\(f\) . u.) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof that Maximum Rate of Change occurs along grad\(f\):) Tj
T*
(   - By definition of dot product:) Tj
T*
(   D_u f = grad\(f\) . u = |grad\(f\)| * |u| * cos\(theta\) = |grad\(f\)| * cos\(theta\),) Tj
T*
(   where theta is the angle between grad\(f\) and unit vector u.) Tj
T*
(   - Since -1 <= cos\(theta\) <= 1:) Tj
T*
(   The maximum value occurs when cos\(theta\) = 1 => theta = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - This means u is in the EXACT direction of grad\(f\): u = grad\(f\) / |grad\(f\)|.) Tj
T*
(   - The maximum directional derivative is strictly |grad\(f\)|. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Numerical Computation for f = x^2 * y * z + 4*x*z^2 at P\(1, -2, -1\):) Tj
T*
(   - Gradient components:) Tj
T*
(   fx = 2*x*y*z + 4*z^2 => fx\(1, -2, -1\) = 2\(1\)\(-2\)\(-1\) + 4\(-1\)^2 = 4 + 4 = 8.) Tj
T*
(   fy = x^2 * z => fy\(1, -2, -1\) = \(1\)^2 * \(-1\) = -1.) Tj
T*
(   fz = x^2 * y + 8*x*z => fz\(1, -2, -1\) = \(1\)^2 * \(-2\) + 8\(1\)\(-1\) = -2 - 8 = -10.) Tj
T*
(   - Gradient vector:) Tj
T*
(   grad\(f\) = 8*i - j - 10*k.) Tj
T*
(   - Direction vector: v = 2*i - j - 2*k.) Tj
T*
(   Magnitude |v| = sqrt\(2^2 + \(-1\)^2 + \(-2\)^2\) = sqrt\(4 + 1 + 4\) = sqrt\(9\) = 3.) Tj
T*
(   Unit vector: u = \(2/3\)*i - \(1/3\)*j - \(2/3\)*k.) Tj
T*
(   - Compute Directional Derivative:) Tj
T*
(   D_u f = grad\(f\) . u = [ 8*\(2/3\) + \(-1\)*\(-1/3\) + \(-10\)*\(-2/3\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = 16/3 + 1/3 + 20/3 = 37 / 3 = 12.333.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The directional derivative is 37 / 3.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
